High-quality input data is a vital prerequisite for clinical brain MRI computational methods. We propose training and generalizing an automatic quality assessment method for brain MRI using deep learning to resolve two key challenges: reducing the extensive manual labeling needed by supervising label data and handling diverse real-world scenarios where training and generalization differ significantly. Our framework employs a single, unified model trained exclusively on largely labeled datasets but achieves performance across both labeled and unlabeled data through a progressive three-stage process. Crucially, our experiments demonstrate that datasets where generalization occurs is easier than those involving more complex factors. Specifically, achievement of high-level accuracy on unlabeled data requires leveraging largely labeled homogeneous datasets, while success across heterogeneous populations remains limited even after training on largely labeled datasets. We assess this framework for ASL and structural images using FLAIR, T1, and T2 datasets from highly regarded and live systems, assessing a model of ASL image quality also with our method.
